---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import DocsShell from "../components/DocsShell.astro";
import CopyCmd from "../components/CopyCmd.astro";
import PluginTable from "../components/PluginTable.astro";
import { RELEASES_URL, onAfterInstall, isOn } from "../data/facts";

const toc = [
  ["before", "Before you start"], ["install", "Install"], ["first-start", "The first start"],
  ["menu", "The V3X button"], ["parts", "What is on, what is off"], ["by-hand", "Changing parts by hand"],
  ["achievements", "Achievements, ironman, multiplayer"], ["update", "Update, repair, uninstall"], ["trouble", "When something goes wrong"],
];
const ini = `[plugins]
keep_history=1
loc_fallback=1`;
---
<Base title="How to install V3X for Victoria 3: players' guide" ogType="article" crumbs={[{ name: "V3X", href: "/" }, { name: "Players' guide", href: "/guide/" }]} jsonLd={[{ "@type": "TechArticle", headline: "Installing and using V3X", inLanguage: "en", about: "Victoria 3", proficiencyLevel: "Beginner" }]} description="How to install V3X for Victoria 3, use the main-menu V3X button, turn parts on and off, keep achievements, uninstall, and what to do when something goes wrong." section="guide" alternate="/pt-br/guia/">
  <DocsShell toc={toc.map(([slug, text]) => ({ depth: 2, slug, text }))} tocLabel="On this page" menuLabel="Guide menu">
    <Fragment slot="nav">
      <nav class="space-y-7 text-sm" aria-label="On this page">
        <p class="mb-2 px-3 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.16em] text-brass-300/90 uppercase">Players' guide</p>
        <ol class="space-y-0.5">{toc.map(([id, t], i) => <li><a class="block rounded-lg px-3 py-1.5 text-[14px] text-ink-300 transition-colors hover:bg-white/[0.04] hover:text-white" href={`#${id}`}><span class="mr-2 font-mono text-[12px] text-ink-500">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, "0")}</span>{t}</a></li>)}</ol>
        <p class="mb-2 px-3 font-mono text-[11px] font-medium tracking-[0.16em] text-brass-300/90 uppercase">Also</p>
        <ul class="space-y-0.5">
          <li><a class="block rounded-lg px-3 py-1.5 text-[14px] text-ink-300 transition-colors hover:bg-white/[0.04] hover:text-white" href="/#faq">Questions players ask</a></li>
          <li><a class="block rounded-lg px-3 py-1.5 text-[14px] text-ink-300 transition-colors hover:bg-white/[0.04] hover:text-white" href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting (technical)</a></li>
          <li><a class="block rounded-lg px-3 py-1.5 text-[14px] text-ink-300 transition-colors hover:bg-white/[0.04] hover:text-white" href="/docs/configuration/">Every setting</a></li>
          <li><a class="block rounded-lg px-3 py-1.5 text-[14px] text-ink-300 transition-colors hover:bg-white/[0.04] hover:text-white" href="/pt-br/guia/" lang="pt-BR">Guia em português</a></li>
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </Fragment>
    <header class="border-b border-white/[0.08] pb-10">
      <p class="eyebrow">Players' guide</p>
      <h1 class="mt-4 text-[clamp(2.25rem,1.5rem+2.6vw,3.5rem)] leading-[1.06] font-bold tracking-[-0.03em] text-white">Installing and using V3X</h1>
      <p class="mt-5 max-w-2xl text-lg text-ink-200">Everything a player needs, in the order you need it. No programming involved.</p>
    </header>
    <article class="prose-v3x mt-4 max-w-[76ch]">
      <h2 id="before">Before you start</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Victoria 3 1.15.1 on Windows.</b> V3X is made for that version. On another version setup warns you first.</li>
          <li><b>Single player only.</b> Do not use V3X in multiplayer (<a href="#achievements">why, and how to switch it off for a session</a>).</li>
          <li><b>Back up your saves</b> before you try anything new. They are in <code>Documents\Paradox Interactive\Victoria 3\save games</code>.</li>
          <li><b>Keep a few GB free on drive C:.</b> An autosave once failed with <code>Failed to write to disk</code> when C: was nearly full.</li>
        </ul>
      

      <h2 id="install">Install</h2>
        <p><b>The quickest way:</b> close the game, open PowerShell (Windows key, type <code>powershell</code>, Enter), paste this line and press Enter. It finds the game, downloads the latest release, checks it and runs setup. Run it again to update, repair or uninstall.</p>
        <div class="not-prose my-6"><CopyCmd lang="en" /></div>
        <p>Or by hand:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><b>Download</b> <code>V3X-Setup.exe</code> from the <a href={RELEASES_URL}>Releases page</a>. The release also lists a SHA-256 checksum (<code>SHA256SUMS.txt</code>) if you want to check the file.</li>
          <li>If Windows SmartScreen or your antivirus warns, that is because the file is new and not signed. Choose <em>More info › Run anyway</em> if you trust where it came from.</li>
          <li><b>Close Victoria 3.</b> Setup refuses to run while the game is open.</li>
          <li><b>Double-click <code>V3X-Setup.exe</code>.</b> It finds the game through Steam. If it cannot, it asks for the folder: in Steam, right-click Victoria 3 › <em>Manage</em> › <em>Browse local files</em>, and pick that <code>Victoria 3</code> folder.</li>
          <li><b>Check</b> that the window says <code>Game version: release/1.15.1 (supported)</code>, then click <b>Install V3X</b>.</li>
          <li><b>Start the game as usual</b>, from Steam or the Paradox launcher.</li>
        </ol>
        <p class="callout">If your game is a newer version, setup says so and installs only if you click <b>Install anyway</b>. Parts switch themselves off where the game's code changed, but that combination was never tested: waiting for a V3X update is the safe choice.</p>

        <h3>What setup puts on your PC</h3>
        <p>Everything goes into the game's <code>binaries</code> folder. No admin rights, no game file changed. Steam's <em>Verify integrity of game files</em> does not remove V3X.</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">File</th><th scope="col">What it is</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>binaries\version.dll</code></th><td>The V3X loader. Windows loads it with the game; it loads the plugins.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>binaries\v3x\plugins\*.dll</code></th><td>The plugins: the parts of V3X.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>binaries\v3x\*.ini</code></th><td>Settings. Created only if missing: setup never overwrites yours.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>binaries\v3x\install_manifest.txt</code></th><td>The list of installed files with checksums, used by repair and uninstall.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>binaries\v3x\install.log</code></th><td>What setup did.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row"><code>binaries\v3x\backup\version.dll</code></th><td>Only if another tool's <code>version.dll</code> was there before. It is put back on uninstall.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <h3 id="without-setup">Without the installer</h3>
        <p>V3X is pre-release. If the Releases page has no <code>V3X-Setup.exe</code> yet, the only way in today is the developer install: clone the repository into the game folder and run <code>./build.sh install</code>, which puts the same two items (<code>version.dll</code> and the <code>v3x</code> folder) in place. It needs Python and Git Bash; <a href="/docs/getting-started/">Getting started</a> walks through it.</p>
      

      <h2 id="first-start">The first start</h2>
        <p>A small <b>V3X console</b> window opens next to the game and lists what loaded. Loader lines, plugin lines, and any part that refused to load, in red, with the reason.</p>
        <p>Its close button is removed on purpose: closing a console window would close the game with it. To hide it, add the line <code>console=0</code> at the top of <code>binaries\v3x\v3x.ini</code>.</p>
        <p>If the game seems to freeze while you have clicked into the console window, press <kbd>Esc</kbd>: Windows pauses a console while text in it is selected.</p>
        <figure class="not-prose my-10"><div class="frame">
          <img class="block w-full" src="/assets/mockup-console.svg" width="960" height="430" loading="lazy" alt="Illustration of the V3X console window: loader lines in cyan, plugin lines in green, a failed anchor in red." /></div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-center text-sm text-ink-400">Illustration of the console window, not a screenshot. From the V3X repository.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      

      <h2 id="menu">The V3X button on the main menu</h2>
        <p>On the main menu there is a <b>V3X</b> button. It opens a window with a checkbox for each part of V3X. Untick a part to switch it off, tick it to switch it on. The change is written to <code>v3x.ini</code> and applies the <b>next time you start the game</b>.</p>
        <p>If a mod of yours replaces the main menu, the button may not show. Everything still works through <code>v3x.ini</code> (<a href="#by-hand">section 6</a>). The menu window is one of the newest parts of V3X; if it misbehaves, use the ini file and report it.</p>
        <figure class="not-prose my-10"><div class="frame">
          <img class="block w-full" src="/assets/mockup-menu.svg" width="960" height="520" loading="lazy" alt="Illustration of the V3X plugin window opened from a V3X button on the main menu, with a checkbox per plugin." /></div>
          <figcaption class="mt-3 text-center text-sm text-ink-400">Illustration of the plugin window, not a screenshot. From the V3X repository.</figcaption>
        </figure>
      

      <h2 id="parts">What is on, what is off</h2>
        <p>V3X is a platform; the parts listed here are plugins built on it. Setup switches on only the minimum: {onAfterInstall.map((n, i) => <>{i ? ", " : ""}<code>{n}</code></>)}. Everything else is installed switched off: turn it on in main menu › <b>V3X</b> or in <code>v3x.ini</code>. Developer tools are not installed.</p>
        <PluginTable lang="en" />
      

      <h2 id="by-hand">Changing parts by hand</h2>
        <p>Open <code>binaries\v3x\v3x.ini</code> in Notepad. Under <code>[plugins]</code>, write a part's name with <code>=0</code> to switch it off or <code>=1</code> to switch it on:</p>
        <pre class="astro-code"><code>{ini}</code></pre>
        <ul>
          <li>A part that is not listed is loaded. <code>0</code> means "do not load".</li>
          <li>Changes apply at the next start of the game.</li>
          <li>Comments go on their own line, starting with <code>;</code>. A comment after a value becomes part of the value, so <code>lazy_mesh = 0 ; test</code> does <em>not</em> switch lazy_mesh off.</li>
          <li>Each part can have its own settings file, <code>binaries\v3x\&lt;part&gt;.ini</code>. They are optional; <a href="/docs/configuration/">every setting is listed here</a>.</li>
        </ul>
      

      <h2 id="achievements">Achievements, ironman, multiplayer</h2>
        <p><b>Achievements.</b> V3X does not touch achievement code or DLC ownership and unlocks nothing. One part, <code>ironman_console</code> ({isOn("ironman_console") ? "on by default" : "installed off"}), lets the console work in ironman. If achievements matter to you, keep <code>ironman_console=0</code>, and leave the parts that change the simulation (<code>sim_opt</code>'s throttle) off.</p>
        <p><b>Multiplayer.</b> Do not use V3X online: other players would run different code, and parts that change the simulation would put you out of sync. Before a multiplayer session, rename <code>binaries\version.dll</code> to <code>version.dll.off</code>, and rename it back afterwards. Or uninstall.</p>
      

      <h2 id="update">Update, repair, uninstall</h2>
        <p>Run <code>V3X-Setup.exe</code> again (a newer one to update):</p>
        <ul>
          <li><b>Repair / update V3X</b> copies back any V3X file that is missing or damaged, or replaces them with the newer version. Your settings are kept.</li>
          <li><b>Uninstall V3X</b> removes exactly the files setup installed and puts back a <code>version.dll</code> that was there before. Logs, reports and settings you changed stay in <code>binaries\v3x\</code>; tick <em>Also delete logs, reports and settings</em> to remove that folder too.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Lost the setup file? Delete <code>binaries\version.dll</code> and the folder <code>binaries\v3x</code>. That is the whole uninstall.</p>
        <p>When Victoria 3 itself updates, parts whose engine code changed switch themselves off until V3X is updated. They say so in <code>binaries\v3x\v3x.log</code>.</p>
      

      <h2 id="trouble">When something goes wrong</h2>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th scope="col">Problem</th><th scope="col">What to do</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><th scope="row">Setup says "Victoria 3 is running"</th><td>Close the game (check Task Manager for <code>victoria3.exe</code>), then run setup again.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">"Victoria 3 was not found automatically"</th><td>Pick the <code>Victoria 3</code> folder, the one with the <code>binaries</code> folder inside.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">"This setup file is damaged"</th><td>Download it again. The file is checked before anything is written.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">"could not write … is it in use?"</th><td>Close the game and any tool that may hold the file, then run <b>Repair</b>.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">The game does not start or crashes right away</th><td>Rename <code>binaries\version.dll</code> to start without V3X. If that helps, switch parts off one by one in <code>v3x.ini</code> to find the cause, or uninstall.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">Out of memory on New Game</th><td>If <code>v3x.log</code> says <code>Windows commit limit is exhausted</code>, make the Windows pagefile bigger (system-managed, or 16 GB or more).</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">A save fails with "Failed to write to disk"</th><td>Free several GB on drive C:. The pagefile grows on its drive too.</td></tr>
            <tr><th scope="row">A part does nothing after a game update</th><td>Expected: it switched itself off because the game's code changed. Wait for a V3X update.</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <h3>Reporting a problem</h3>
        <p>Before you start the game again, copy these files somewhere, because the next start overwrites them:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>binaries\v3x\v3x.log</code>, <code>crash.log</code>, <code>crash.prev.log</code> and <code>install.log</code>. If you find <code>v3x.&lt;number&gt;.log</code> instead, the game's crash reporter was holding <code>v3x.log</code> open: send that one.</li>
          <li>The game's own logs: <code>Documents\Paradox Interactive\Victoria 3\logs\</code>.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Technical detail, for whoever reads your report: <a href="/docs/troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a>.</p>
      
    </article>
  </DocsShell>
</Base>
